WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.980
In my last video on "Parasite," I talked about the film's visual architecture,

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:07.960
focusing on production design and the spatial layout of the world.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:12.500
I touched a bit on how cinematography was used to help create that structure,

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:18.400
but there are many other ways that cinematography plays a significant role in shaping the story.

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.280
So in this video, let's take a broader look at "Parasite"'s cinematography,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:29.260
the use of camera movement and composition, to help pace the film, keep us engaged, and illustrate its themes.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.480
This video is sponsored by Audible.

00:00:36.580 --> 00:00:40.800
In the first half of the film, as the Kim family moves deeper and deeper into their con,

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.780
the camera consistently, slowly pushes forward,

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.560
almost barely perceptible in many of these shots,

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:51.080
but easily noticeable if I speed it up.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:58.660
This slow creep forward gives each of these moments a foreboding sense of progression towards something.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:31.360
Once the Kims achieve their goal and have status in the house, the camera stops moving forward.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:35.500
Movement forward and backward becomes much more static,

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:41.080
and is only occasionally used through the rest of the film to emphasize specific moments.

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:06.740
As the con develops,

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:11.400
Bong Joon-Ho uses visual cues to connect similar moments in the film:

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:16.340
A slow pan marks the escalation of the con in both of these scenes.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:19.460
This slow pan was used at the beginning of the film

00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:23.880
when Min pitches the idea of conning the Parks to Ki-Woo

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:48.820
A similar pan is used again when Mr. Kim begins pitching his wife as a housekeeper to Mr. Park.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.880
Parasite only has about 960 shots,

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:56.800
a bit below the average of 1250.

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.340
But the pacing of the film actually feels quite quick.

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:04.920
In your average Hollywood film, you might see this scene progress like this,

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.620
cutting between angles.

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:09.780
But Bong often uses longer takes without cutting.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:13.880
These takes rarely feel very long because they aren't static:

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:18.440
Bong uses developing composition and camerawork to incorporate wide,

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:19.640
medium,

00:03:19.640 --> 00:03:22.060
and close-up shots into a single take.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.920
Bong's immaculate storyboarding and shot planning

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:30.760
allows him to use camera movement and blocking in concert to help develop scenes.

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:36.620
In this scene, Mr. Park's movement towards the trashcan is what motivates the horizontal camera dolly.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:42.620
But that movement continues, and is what ends up revealing to the audience a new element of the scene.

00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.860
In the film, more free-flowing steadicam shots are used within the big house

00:03:49.860 --> 00:03:54.540
to emphasize the size of the space and the freedom in the rich family's lifestyle.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:01.800
In the Kim's house, shots are often more locked-down and simple.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:06.700
But as the Kims start to see the benefit of their new jobs in their lives,

00:04:06.700 --> 00:04:10.600
the camera becomes freer and more complex in its movement.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:18.580
The film's static tripod shots, precise pans, steady dolly shots, and flowing steadicam tracking shots

00:04:18.580 --> 00:04:22.760
are exchanged for handheld shots at its climax.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:25.600
Though these shots are handheld and slightly more unsteady,

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:27.880
they're no less precisely composed,

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:32.340
often including multiple careful compositions in a single take.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.720
By reserving handheld for these moments,

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:39.200
Bong maximizes its effect in contrast to the steadier

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:41.820
cinematography of the rest of the film.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:48.360
"Parasite"'s composition uses the set to help reinforce one of its themes

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:36.820
None of these techniques were specifically pioneered by Bong Joon-Ho,

00:06:36.820 --> 00:06:40.580
and "Parasite" isn't even the first time he's used most of them.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:44.180
But Bong displays a mastery and ease with the camera

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:46.960
in bringing his vision to the screen that's admirable.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:50.160
There's clear intention behind every shot.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:54.160
But wonderfully, these things don't draw attention to themselves.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:58.140
Bong's long takes or well-choreographed tracking shots

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:03.060
aren't there for show, but because they serve each moment of the story best.

